'''bemitered, bemitred''' (bẹ̄-mī′tėrd), ''a.'' [< ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''miter'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Crowned with or wearing a miter. ''Carlyle.''

'''bemoan''' (bẹ̄-mōn′), ''v. t.'' [< ME. (with change of vowel; cf. ''moan'') ''bemenen'', ''bimenen'', < AS. ''bemǣnan'', bemoan, < ''be-'' + ''mǣnan'', moan: see ''be-''<sup>1</sup> and ''moan''.] '''1.''' To lament; bewail; express sorrow for: as, to ''bemoan'' the loss of a son.—'''2.''' Reflexively, to bewail one’s lot.
{{smaller block|People grieve and ''bemoan themselves'', but it is not half so bad with them as they say.
{{right|''Emerson'', Experience.}}}}
'''3†.''' To pity; feel or express sympathy with or pity for.
{{smaller block|Bastards, . . . if proving eminent, are much ''bemoaned'', because merely passive in the blemish of their birth.
{{right|''Fuller.''}}}}

'''bemoanable''' (bẹ̄-mō′na̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''bemoan'' + ''-able''.] Capable or worthy of being lamented. ''Sherwood.''

'''bemoaner''' (bẹ̄-mō′nėr), ''n.'' One who bemoans.

'''bemock''' (bẹ̄-mok′), ''v. t.'' [< ''be-''<sup>1</sup> + ''mock''.] '''1.''' To mock repeatedly; flout.
{{smaller block|Have we not seen him disappointed, ''bemocked'' of Destiny, through long years?
{{right|''Carlyle'', Sartor Resartus, p. 111.}}}}
'''2.''' To cause to appear mock or unreal; excel or surpass, as the genuine surpasses the counterfeit.
{{smaller block|<poem>Her beams ''bemocked'' the sultry main
Like April hoar-frost spread.</poem>
{{right|''Coleridge'', Anc. Mariner, iv.}}
<poem>A laugh which in the woodland rang,</poem>}}

